Let $\mathcal{O}_X$ be the sheaf of functions on a smooth affine variety $X$. Then, for every Zariski open set $U$ in $X$, there is an isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{O}_X(U)\otimes_{\mathbb C}\mathcal{O}_X(U)\cong\mathcal{O}_{X\times X}(U\times U)\quad(1).
\end{equation}
My question is what a section in $(\mathcal{O}_X\otimes_{\mathbb C}\mathcal{O}_X)(U)\cong \mathcal{O}_X(U)\otimes_{\mathbb C}\mathcal{O}_X(U)$ looks like? Is it a function
\begin{align}
f\otimes g: U\times U&\to \mathbb C\quad(2)\\
&(x, y)\mapsto f(x)g(y)
\end{align}
or is it somehow a function
\begin{align}
f\otimes g: \Delta_U\subset U\times U&\to \mathbb C\quad(3)\\
&(x, x)\mapsto f(x)g(x),
\end{align}
where $\Delta_U$ is the diagonal is $U\times U$ and $(x, y)$ is an arbitrary point on the product $U\times U$.
Isomorphism $(1)$ suggests that a section in $(\mathcal{O}_X\otimes_{\mathbb C}\mathcal{O}_X)(U)$ is of the form $(2)$ but the fact that $f\otimes g$ is an element of the space $(\mathcal{O}_X\otimes\mathcal{O}_X)(U)$ confuses me. Someone might be able to provide an insight on this basic issue.
Ultimately, as the tensor product of sheaves is just a presheaf and we need to sheafify, a section of the sheaf $\mathcal{O}_X\otimes\mathcal{O}_X$ over a Zariski open set $U$ in $X$ might be a map
\begin{align}
s:U\to \coprod_{x\in U}\mathcal{O}_{X, x}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{X, x}\\
x\mapsto s_x:=f_x\otimes g_x
\end{align}
